[Event "Rosenwald Memorial, New York"]
[Site "New York USA"]
[Date "1956.10.17"]
[Round "8"]
[White "Donald Byrne"]
[Black "Robert J. Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "D97"]
[Themes "attack,initiative,queen-sacrifice,sacrifice"]
[Level "4"]
[Why "The 'Game of the Century'. A 13-year-old Fischer gives up his queen for a swarm of minor pieces and rooks that cooperate better than White's lone queen. It is the clearest demonstration that material is only one currency: activity, tempo and coordination are the others. Also a model of a 'windmill' of discovered checks."]

1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. d4 O-O 5. Bf4 d5 6. Qb3 dxc4 7. Qxc4 c6 8. e4 Nbd7 9. Rd1 Nb6 10. Qc5 Bg4 11. Bg5 Na4 { Key: a deflection: if 12.Qxa4 Nxe4 and the g5-bishop hangs with threats on c3. } 12. Qa3 Nxc3 13. bxc3 Nxe4 { Key: Fischer keeps grabbing material while every White piece is overloaded. } 14. Bxe7 Qb6 15. Bc4 Nxc3 16. Bc5 Rfe8+ 17. Kf1 Be6 { Key: the queen sacrifice; 18.Bxb6 Bxc4+ 19.Kg1 Ne2+ 20.Kf1 Nxd4+ 21.Kg1 Ne2+ 22.Kf1 Nc3+ - the windmill wins the queen's worth of material back. } 18. Bxb6 Bxc4+ 19. Kg1 Ne2+ 20. Kf1 Nxd4+ 21. Kg1 Ne2+ 22. Kf1 Nc3+ 23. Kg1 axb6 24. Qb4 Ra4 25. Qxb6 Nxd1 { Key: Black has rook, two bishops and a knight for the queen; from here White is simply lost. } 26. h3 Rxa2 27. Kh2 Nxf2 28. Re1 Rxe1 29. Qd8+ Bf8 30. Nxe1 Bd5 31. Nf3 Ne4 32. Qb8 b5 33. h4 h5 34. Ne5 Kg7 35. Kg1 Bc5+ 36. Kf1 Ng3+ 37. Ke1 Bb4+ 38. Kd1 Bb3+ 39. Kc1 Ne2+ 40. Kb1 Nc3+ 41. Kc1 Rc2# { Key: the king is hunted from g1 to c1 and mated by rook and minor pieces. } 0-1

[Event "US Championship 1963/64, New York"]
[Site "New York USA"]
[Date "1963.12.18"]
[Round "3"]
[White "Robert Byrne"]
[Black "Robert J. Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "D71"]
[Themes "attack,initiative,sacrifice"]
[Level "4"]
[Why "Fischer's attack against a solid Grunfeld structure ends with a quiet move that the commentators analysing in the press room thought was winning for White. It teaches that an attack on a castled king can be prepared by positional means (...Nd3, ...Ba6 hitting the light squares) and finished with a sacrifice."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 c6 4. Bg2 d5 5. cxd5 cxd5 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. e3 O-O 8. Nge2 Nc6 9. O-O b6 10. b3 Ba6 11. Ba3 Re8 12. Qd2 e5 13. dxe5 Nxe5 14. Rfd1 Nd3 { Key: the knight invades; White's king position (g2-bishop, e3 pawn) is about to crack. } 15. Qc2 Nxf2 { Key: two knight blows remove the defenders of the light squares. } 16. Kxf2 Ng4+ 17. Kg1 Nxe3 18. Qd2 Nxg2 { Key: the second sacrifice opens the long diagonal for the a6-bishop and the queen. } 19. Kxg2 d4 20. Nxd4 { Key: the quiet move: ...Qh3+ cannot be stopped (22.Qf2 Qh3+ 23.Kg1 Re1+!! 24.Rxe1 Bxd4 and mate). } 20... Bb7+ 21. Kf1 Qd7 0-1

[Event "Candidates semi-final (game 10), Bled"]
[Site "Bled YUG"]
[Date "1965.??.??"]
[Round "10"]
[White "Mikhail Tal"]
[Black "Bent Larsen"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B82"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Scheveningen Variation, Tal Variation"]
[Themes "attack,initiative,sacrifice,pawn-storm,opposite-castling"]
[Level "4"]
[Why "The decisive game of the Tal-Larsen match and the standard example of a knight sacrifice on d5 in the Sicilian: Tal gives a piece for two central pawns and an open king, then follows with a second sacrifice. Study how both sides race pawn storms after castling on opposite wings and how Tal keeps the initiative without immediate mate."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nc3 d6 6. Be3 Nf6 7. f4 Be7 8. Qf3 O-O 9. O-O-O { Key: opposite-side castling; White starts the g-pawn storm at once. } 9... Qc7 10. Ndb5 Qb8 11. g4 a6 12. Nd4 Nxd4 13. Bxd4 b5 14. g5 { Key: Larsen's counter-storm arrives one tempo too late. } 14... Nd7 15. Bd3 b4 16. Nd5 { Key: the thematic piece sacrifice: the e-file opens, the king on g8 feels the d3-bishop and the h4/h5 ideas. } 16... exd5 17. exd5 f5 18. Rde1 Rf7 19. h4 Bb7 20. Bxf5 { Key: a second sacrifice; every Black piece is now overloaded. } 20... Rxf5 21. Rxe7 Ne5 22. Qe4 Qf8 23. fxe5 { Key: Tal regains material with a crushing position. } 23... Rf4 24. Qe3 Rf3 25. Qe2 Qxe7 26. Qxf3 dxe5 27. Re1 Rd8 28. Rxe5 Qd6 29. Qf4 Rf8 30. Qe4 b3 31. axb3 Rf1+ 32. Kd2 Qb4+ 33. c3 Qd6 34. Bc5 Qxc5 35. Re8+ Rf8 36. Qe6+ Kh8 37. Qf7 { Key: mate on g7/h7 is unstoppable. } 1-0

