[Why "A queen sacrifice that does not mate: Tal gives the queen for two minor pieces and a lasting initiative, then wins a long endgame. It teaches that the value of a sacrifice is measured by the resulting activity, not by immediate tactics, and shows the technique needed after the fireworks."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. Nc3 Bb4 5. Bg5 Bb7 6. e3 h6 7. Bh4 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 d6 9. Nd2 e5 10. f3 Qe7 11. e4 Nbd7 12. Bd3 Nf8 13. c5 { Key: Tal gives a pawn to open the position with Black's king still in the centre. } 13... dxc5 14. dxe5 Qxe5 15. Qa4+ c6 16. O-O Ng6 17. Nc4 { Key: the queen is given up for a pawn storm against the king. } 17... Qe6 18. e5 b5 19. exf6 bxa4 20. fxg7 Rg8 21. Bf5 { Key: material is now roughly level (two rooks and two minor pieces each), but every White piece attacks and Black's king is in the open. } 21... Nxh4 22. Bxe6 Ba6 23. Nd6+ Ke7 24. Bc4 Rxg7 25. g3 Kxd6 26. Bxa6 Nf5 27. Rab1 f6 28. Rfd1+ Ke7 29. Re1+ Kd6 30. Kf2 c4 31. g4 Ne7 32. Rb7 Rag8 33. Bxc4 Nd5 34. Bxd5 cxd5 35. Rb4 Rc8 36. Rxa4 { Key: the game reaches a rook ending where White's g-pawn decides. } 36... Rxc3 37. Ra6+ Kc5 38. Rxf6 h5 39. h3 hxg4 40. hxg4 Rh7 41. g5 Rh5 42. Rf5 Rc2+ 43. Kg3 Kc4 44. Ree5 d4 45. g6 { Key: the passed g-pawn costs Black the rook. } 45... Rh1 46. Rc5+ Kd3 47. Rxc2 Kxc2 48. Kf4 Rg1 49. Rg5 1-0

