[Why "Down 6-5 with three games left, Ding wins a double-edged game: he gives up the bishop pair to damage Black's kingside (12.Bxf6 gxf6), weathers Nepomniachtchi's h-file attack and strikes in the centre and on the queenside. The decisive mistake 34...f5? is answered by 35.Rxe6! and a passed d-pawn."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 d5 3. e3 c5 4. Nbd2 cxd4 5. exd4 Qc7 6. c3 Bd7 7. Bd3 Nc6 8. O-O Bg4 9. Re1 e6 10. Nf1 Bd6 11. Bg5 { Key: both sides accept weakened kings; White gets the h5-knight and the g-file. } 11... O-O 12. Bxf6 gxf6 13. Ng3 f5 14. h3 Bxf3 15. Qxf3 Ne7 16. Nh5 Kh8 17. g4 Rg8 18. Kh1 Ng6 19. Bc2 Nh4 20. Qe3 { Key: Nepomniachtchi piles up on the g-file; Ding opens the queenside and centre. } 20... Rg6 21. Rg1 f4 22. Qd3 Qe7 23. Rae1 Qg5 24. c4 dxc4 25. Qc3 b5 26. a4 b4 27. Qxc4 Rag8 28. Qc6 { Key: the attack is one tempo short; 34...f5 opens the e-file for White. } 28... Bb8 29. Qb7 Rh6 30. Be4 Rf8 31. Qxb4 Qd8 32. Qc3 Ng6 33. Bg2 Qh4 34. Re2 f5 35. Rxe6 { Key: the passed d-pawn and the e6-rook decide. } 35... Rxh5 36. gxh5 Qxh5 37. d5+ Kg8 38. d6 1-0

