[Why "The game that shook Kasparov: Deep Blue declined a pawn (37.Be4!! instead of 37.Qb6) and played a slow positional squeeze. Kasparov resigned on move 45 in a position later shown to be a draw by perpetual check (45...Qe3! 46.Qxd6 Re8!). Two lessons: a machine can play 'human' positional chess, and never resign before checking all the checks."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 h6 10. d4 Re8 11. Nbd2 Bf8 12. Nf1 Bd7 13. Ng3 Na5 14. Bc2 c5 15. b3 { Key: the closed Ruy Lopez; White's a-file and the f4 break give a slow bind. } 15... Nc6 16. d5 Ne7 17. Be3 Ng6 18. Qd2 Nh7 19. a4 Nh4 20. Nxh4 Qxh4 21. Qe2 Qd8 22. b4 Qc7 23. Rec1 c4 24. Ra3 Rec8 25. Rca1 Qd8 26. f4 Nf6 { Key: White trades Black's defenders and opens the a-file. } 27. fxe5 dxe5 28. Qf1 Ne8 29. Qf2 Nd6 30. Bb6 Qe8 31. R3a2 Be7 32. Bc5 Bf8 33. Nf5 Bxf5 34. exf5 f6 35. Bxd6 Bxd6 36. axb5 axb5 37. Be4 { Key: Kasparov resigned; 45...Qe3! 46.Qxd6 Re8! 47.h4 h5! holds a perpetual (analysis after the game). } 37... Rxa2 38. Qxa2 Qd7 39. Qa7 Rc7 40. Qb6 Rb7 41. Ra8+ Kf7 42. Qa6 Qc7 43. Qc6 Qb6+ 44. Kf1 Rb8 45. Ra6 1-0

